1
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:12,680
In the 1920s,

2
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:14,560
one of the best jobs a young woman could

3
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,240
get in America came with a strange side effect:

4
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,946
she glowed in the dark

5
00:00:19,096 --> 00:00:19,566
Her hair,

6
00:00:19,566 --> 00:00:20,126
her dress,

7
00:00:20,126 --> 00:00:22,726
even her smile shone with a soft green light

8
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:24,774
Her bosses told her the glowing paint was

9
00:00:24,774 --> 00:00:25,654
perfectly safe

10
00:00:25,654 --> 00:00:26,793
It was not

11
00:00:26,943 --> 00:00:29,131
And some of the people in charge knew

12
00:00:29,281 --> 00:00:31,631
This is the story of the Radium Girls,

13
00:00:31,631 --> 00:00:33,431
the young women who were poisoned at work,

14
00:00:33,431 --> 00:00:35,511
and then fought back

15
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:40,320
In 1898,

16
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:43,160
Marie and Pierre Curie discovered a new element

17
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:44,520
that seemed almost magical

18
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:45,080
It glowed,

19
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,680
and it gave off energy all by itself

20
00:00:47,994 --> 00:00:49,949
They called it radium

21
00:00:50,099 --> 00:00:51,049
Within a few years,

22
00:00:51,049 --> 00:00:52,749
radium became a craze

23
00:00:52,749 --> 00:00:55,009
Companies sold radium tonics,

24
00:00:55,009 --> 00:00:56,289
radium water,

25
00:00:56,289 --> 00:00:59,009
even radium toothpaste and face creams,

26
00:00:59,009 --> 00:01:01,089
all promising better health

27
00:01:01,246 --> 00:01:03,636
One company mixed radium into a paint that

28
00:01:03,636 --> 00:01:04,516
glowed in the dark

29
00:01:04,516 --> 00:01:06,472
They called it Undark

30
00:01:06,622 --> 00:01:07,692
Painted on watch faces,

31
00:01:07,692 --> 00:01:09,451
it let people read the time at night

32
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:12,051
All it needed was someone to paint it

33
00:01:15,417 --> 00:01:16,887
In 1917,

34
00:01:16,887 --> 00:01:19,207
the United States entered the First World War,

35
00:01:19,207 --> 00:01:21,567
and the army needed glowing dials for watches

36
00:01:21,567 --> 00:01:23,007
and instruments

37
00:01:23,231 --> 00:01:23,821
In Orange,

38
00:01:23,821 --> 00:01:24,581
New Jersey,

39
00:01:24,581 --> 00:01:27,061
a radium company hired hundreds of young women

40
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:27,701
to paint them

41
00:01:27,701 --> 00:01:29,746
Many were still teenagers

42
00:01:29,896 --> 00:01:30,966
The pay was excellent,

43
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:33,566
much better than most factory jobs

44
00:01:33,751 --> 00:01:34,661
The work was clean,

45
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:35,941
the girls were friends,

46
00:01:35,941 --> 00:01:38,541
and they were proud to help the war effort

47
00:01:38,714 --> 00:01:40,024
It felt like a dream job

48
00:01:40,024 --> 00:01:41,584
But the way they were taught to paint would

49
00:01:41,584 --> 00:01:43,024
change everything

50
00:01:46,617 --> 00:01:48,567
The numbers on a watch dial are tiny,

51
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:51,167
so the women used very fine brushes

52
00:01:51,459 --> 00:01:52,449
To keep the tip sharp,

53
00:01:52,449 --> 00:01:53,849
they were taught a simple trick:

54
00:01:53,849 --> 00:01:54,249
lip,

55
00:01:54,249 --> 00:01:54,729
dip,

56
00:01:54,729 --> 00:01:55,744
paint

57
00:01:55,894 --> 00:01:57,324
Shape the brush with your lips

58
00:01:57,324 --> 00:01:58,564
Dip it in the glowing paint

59
00:01:58,564 --> 00:01:59,364
Paint a number

60
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:00,324
Then do it again,

61
00:02:00,324 --> 00:02:02,374
hundreds of times a day

62
00:02:02,524 --> 00:02:03,114
Every time,

63
00:02:03,114 --> 00:02:05,714
they swallowed a little bit of radium

64
00:02:06,174 --> 00:02:08,324
Some even painted their nails and teeth for

65
00:02:08,324 --> 00:02:08,604
fun,

66
00:02:08,604 --> 00:02:10,425
to glow at parties

67
00:02:10,575 --> 00:02:11,485
No one stopped them

68
00:02:11,485 --> 00:02:12,085
In fact,

69
00:02:12,085 --> 00:02:14,885
many people back then believed radium was good

70
00:02:14,885 --> 00:02:16,325
for you

71
00:02:19,483 --> 00:02:21,033
When the women asked if the paint was

72
00:02:21,033 --> 00:02:21,593
dangerous,

73
00:02:21,593 --> 00:02:23,393
the answer was always the same:

74
00:02:23,393 --> 00:02:23,713
no,

75
00:02:23,713 --> 00:02:25,473
it's perfectly safe

76
00:02:25,834 --> 00:02:28,304
Yet the scientists who handled pure radium were

77
00:02:28,304 --> 00:02:30,384
careful to protect themselves

78
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:31,500
Even at the time,

79
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:33,740
there were warnings that radium could badly

80
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:35,287
damage the body

81
00:02:35,437 --> 00:02:36,667
But the dials kept selling,

82
00:02:36,667 --> 00:02:38,427
and the brushes kept going in and out of

83
00:02:38,427 --> 00:02:40,082
the women's mouths

84
00:02:40,232 --> 00:02:40,502
Then,

85
00:02:40,502 --> 00:02:41,302
one by one,

86
00:02:41,302 --> 00:02:43,902
the young painters started to get sick

87
00:02:47,483 --> 00:02:49,153
One of the first was Mollie Maggia

88
00:02:49,153 --> 00:02:51,084
It started with a toothache

89
00:02:51,234 --> 00:02:52,664
The dentist pulled the tooth,

90
00:02:52,664 --> 00:02:54,024
but the wound would not heal

91
00:02:54,024 --> 00:02:55,224
Then another tooth went,

92
00:02:55,224 --> 00:02:56,552
and another

93
00:02:56,702 --> 00:02:58,012
Her bones were breaking down,

94
00:02:58,012 --> 00:03:00,062
and no treatment worked

95
00:03:00,212 --> 00:03:02,322
Mollie died in 1922

96
00:03:02,322 --> 00:03:04,402
She was just twenty-four

97
00:03:04,646 --> 00:03:06,166
Doctors blamed another disease

98
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:08,086
It would take years to prove what had really

99
00:03:08,086 --> 00:03:09,273
killed her

100
00:03:09,423 --> 00:03:12,023
And Mollie was only the first

101
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:17,550
Here's why radium was so deadly

102
00:03:17,830 --> 00:03:20,260
The body treats radium a lot like calcium,

103
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:22,020
the mineral that builds our bones

104
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:23,660
So instead of passing through,

105
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,109
it gets stored in the skeleton

106
00:03:26,259 --> 00:03:26,409
There,

107
00:03:26,409 --> 00:03:28,209
it keeps giving off radiation,

108
00:03:28,209 --> 00:03:29,249
year after year,

109
00:03:29,249 --> 00:03:31,089
damaging the bone and the blood from the

110
00:03:31,089 --> 00:03:32,209
inside

111
00:03:32,431 --> 00:03:34,431
Many of the women felt fine for years after

112
00:03:34,431 --> 00:03:35,311
they stopped painting

113
00:03:35,311 --> 00:03:36,884
Then they fell ill

114
00:03:37,034 --> 00:03:38,544
When doctors finally tested them,

115
00:03:38,544 --> 00:03:40,424
some of the women were so radioactive that

116
00:03:40,424 --> 00:03:42,264
their breath and their bones could be measured

117
00:03:42,264 --> 00:03:43,704
with instruments

118
00:03:47,183 --> 00:03:48,573
By 1924,

119
00:03:48,573 --> 00:03:51,173
dozens of former dial painters were sick

120
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:53,460
The company hired a team from Harvard,

121
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:54,900
led by Cecil Drinker,

122
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,694
to study the factory

123
00:03:56,844 --> 00:03:58,074
Their conclusion was clear:

124
00:03:58,074 --> 00:04:00,234
radium was very likely the cause,

125
00:04:00,234 --> 00:04:02,592
and the workers needed protection

126
00:04:02,742 --> 00:04:04,732
But the report given to state officials said

127
00:04:04,732 --> 00:04:05,892
something very different:

128
00:04:05,892 --> 00:04:08,492
that every girl was in perfect condition

129
00:04:08,664 --> 00:04:10,774
A doctor named Alice Hamilton discovered the

130
00:04:10,774 --> 00:04:12,787
switch and helped expose it

131
00:04:12,937 --> 00:04:13,407
Meanwhile,

132
00:04:13,407 --> 00:04:15,407
the company argued that the women had other

133
00:04:15,407 --> 00:04:15,967
illnesses

134
00:04:15,967 --> 00:04:18,367
The truth was being buried

135
00:04:21,717 --> 00:04:23,707
One former painter refused to stay quiet

136
00:04:23,707 --> 00:04:25,910
Her name was Grace Fryer

137
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:27,530
She decided to sue the company

138
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:30,101
But lawyer after lawyer turned her down

139
00:04:30,251 --> 00:04:31,921
It took her two years to find one who

140
00:04:31,921 --> 00:04:32,761
would take the case:

141
00:04:32,761 --> 00:04:35,257
a young lawyer named Raymond Berry

142
00:04:35,407 --> 00:04:36,997
In 1927,

143
00:04:36,997 --> 00:04:39,077
Grace and four other women went to court

144
00:04:39,077 --> 00:04:40,197
together

145
00:04:40,353 --> 00:04:42,343
The newspapers called them the women doomed to

146
00:04:42,343 --> 00:04:42,623
die

147
00:04:42,623 --> 00:04:45,023
They were in their twenties

148
00:04:47,950 --> 00:04:49,980
The company's lawyers had a cold strategy:

149
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:51,100
delay

150
00:04:51,271 --> 00:04:53,261
The law gave workers only two years to sue,

151
00:04:53,261 --> 00:04:55,858
and the women were getting weaker every month

152
00:04:56,008 --> 00:04:57,718
When the hearing was pushed back again,

153
00:04:57,718 --> 00:05:00,318
newspapers across the country were furious

154
00:05:00,955 --> 00:05:02,745
In June 1928,

155
00:05:02,745 --> 00:05:05,078
the company settled out of court

156
00:05:05,228 --> 00:05:07,338
Each woman received ten thousand dollars,

157
00:05:07,338 --> 00:05:09,338
a yearly payment and her medical costs

158
00:05:09,338 --> 00:05:11,653
But it was too late to save them

159
00:05:11,803 --> 00:05:12,633
And in another state,

160
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:15,233
the same story was starting again

161
00:05:18,717 --> 00:05:19,387
In Ottawa,

162
00:05:19,387 --> 00:05:20,187
Illinois,

163
00:05:20,187 --> 00:05:21,227
another company,

164
00:05:21,227 --> 00:05:22,587
Radium Dial,

165
00:05:22,587 --> 00:05:25,187
also hired young women to paint dials

166
00:05:25,567 --> 00:05:26,997
When news came from New Jersey,

167
00:05:26,997 --> 00:05:29,357
the managers told them their radium was

168
00:05:29,357 --> 00:05:30,968
different and safe

169
00:05:31,118 --> 00:05:33,548
Catherine Donohue started painting there at

170
00:05:33,548 --> 00:05:34,108
nineteen

171
00:05:34,108 --> 00:05:34,828
Years later,

172
00:05:34,828 --> 00:05:35,868
she began to limp,

173
00:05:35,868 --> 00:05:38,468
and in 1931 she was fired

174
00:05:39,291 --> 00:05:39,561
She sued

175
00:05:39,561 --> 00:05:41,321
By 1938,

176
00:05:41,321 --> 00:05:43,241
she was too sick to leave her home,

177
00:05:43,241 --> 00:05:44,961
so part of the hearing was held at her

178
00:05:44,961 --> 00:05:46,081
bedside

179
00:05:46,316 --> 00:05:48,066
In April 1938,

180
00:05:48,066 --> 00:05:48,506
she won

181
00:05:48,506 --> 00:05:50,880
The company was held responsible

182
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:52,580
Catherine died a few months later

183
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:55,180
But her victory helped change the law

184
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:01,220
The Radium Girls' cases helped establish that

185
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,180
companies are responsible for the health of

186
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:04,533
their workers

187
00:06:04,713 --> 00:06:06,793
Their bodies also taught scientists how

188
00:06:06,793 --> 00:06:08,873
radiation behaves inside us

189
00:06:09,548 --> 00:06:10,898
In the 1940s,

190
00:06:10,898 --> 00:06:13,138
the scientists of the Manhattan Project used

191
00:06:13,138 --> 00:06:15,138
what was learned from the dial painters to set

192
00:06:15,138 --> 00:06:17,578
safety limits for workers handling radioactive

193
00:06:17,578 --> 00:06:18,698
materials

194
00:06:19,011 --> 00:06:21,361
Their story fed into decades of new workplace

195
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:22,201
safety rules,

196
00:06:22,201 --> 00:06:24,221
all the way to the creation of America's

197
00:06:24,221 --> 00:06:26,621
workplace safety agency in 1970

198
00:06:28,218 --> 00:06:28,848
Without them,

199
00:06:28,848 --> 00:06:31,448
many more people would have been hurt

200
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:36,880
Radium paint was slowly phased out,

201
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,353
and it disappeared from watches by the late

202
00:06:39,353 --> 00:06:40,473
1960s

203
00:06:40,946 --> 00:06:41,216
Today,

204
00:06:41,216 --> 00:06:43,656
glowing watch hands use much safer materials

205
00:06:43,656 --> 00:06:45,893
that soak up light and glow later

206
00:06:46,043 --> 00:06:47,193
In 2011,

207
00:06:47,193 --> 00:06:49,593
the town of Ottawa unveiled a statue of a

208
00:06:49,593 --> 00:06:50,633
young dial painter,

209
00:06:50,633 --> 00:06:52,244
brush in hand

210
00:06:52,394 --> 00:06:54,184
It honours the women who were told everything

211
00:06:54,184 --> 00:06:54,744
was fine,

212
00:06:54,744 --> 00:06:56,864
and who refused to stay silent when it was

213
00:06:56,864 --> 00:06:57,984
not

214
00:06:58,617 --> 00:06:59,927
They were promised a dream job

215
00:06:59,927 --> 00:07:01,007
They were given poison,

216
00:07:01,007 --> 00:07:02,334
and then lies

217
00:07:02,484 --> 00:07:04,234
But the Radium Girls fought back,

218
00:07:04,234 --> 00:07:06,674
and the rules that protect workers today carry

219
00:07:06,674 --> 00:07:07,595
their mark

220
00:07:07,745 --> 00:07:09,955
Had you heard of the Radium Girls before this

221
00:07:09,955 --> 00:07:10,295
video?

222
00:07:10,295 --> 00:07:11,787
Tell me in the comments

223
00:07:11,937 --> 00:07:14,537
Subscribe so you don't miss the next episode
